Creating a graphical user interface (GUI) for your Java application is an essential part of building a user-friendly application. Java provides several libraries to help you create GUIs:

1. **JavaFX**: A modern, high-level library that supports creating applications with 2D and 3D graphics.

2. **Swing**: The default GUI library used in all versions of Java before JavaFX (e.g., Swing, JFC/Swing).

Here is a simple example using `Swing` to create a basic GUI:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create the frame that will hold all of our components.
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Hello World Example");

        // Set the size and title for the frame.
        frame.setSize(200, 150);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        // Add a button to the frame. When it is clicked, it will close the window.
        JButton button = new JButton("Click Me!");
        frame.add(button); // add our button to the frame

        // Show the frame on screen.
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

### Explanation:

- **JFrame**: This class represents a frame, which is a container for other components like `JButton`, `JTextField`, etc.

- **setSize() and setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE)**: These methods set the size of the window and specify what happens when the user closes it. In this example, it will close the application if you close the window by clicking on the "X" or using the Windows taskbar button.

- **add()**: This method adds a component to the frame's layout. Here we add a `JButton` named "Click Me!".

### Advanced GUI Options

In more complex applications, consider these advanced features:

1. **Swing Layout Managers**:
   - `BorderLayout`: Places components in four sections and puts them side by side.
   - `GridBagLayout`: Flexible grid to organize components with constraints for precise control over their placement.

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create the frame that will hold all of our components.
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Hello World Example");

        // Set the size and title for the frame.
        frame.setSize(200, 150);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        // Add a button to the frame. When it is clicked, it will close the window.
        JButton button = new JButton("Click Me!");
        frame.add(button); // add our button to the frame

        // Use BorderLayout for simple layout.
        frame.setLayout(new BorderLayout());
        frame.add(button, "Center");  // Center component on all borders

        // Show the frame on screen.
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

2. **Swing Event Handling**:
   - `ActionEvent`: Triggered when a button is clicked.

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create the frame that will hold all of our components.
        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Hello World Example");

        // Set the size and title for the frame.
        frame.setSize(200, 150);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        // Add a button to the frame. When it is clicked, it will close the window.
        JButton button = new JButton("Click Me!");
        frame.add(button); // add our button to the frame

        // Add an action listener for when the button is clicked
        button.addActionListener(new ActionListener() {
            @Override
            public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
                System.out.println("Button Clicked");
                frame.setVisible(false);
            }
        });

        // Show the frame on screen.
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
```

